'use strict';

window.TUTORIALS_DATA_CONTENT = {
    id: 'content',
    title: 'Content Creation',
    topics: [
        {
            id: 'worlds-overview',
            title: 'Worlds',
            content: "# Worlds\n\nA **World** is the foundational setting for your chats. It defines the universe your characters inhabit.\n\n## What a World Contains\n\n- **Title** — The world's name.\n- **Details** — A description of the setting, history, and rules.\n- **Images** — Visual references for the world. You can add multiple images and set a thumbnail.\n- **Scenarios** — Story situations that take place in this world.\n- **Characters** — People, creatures, or entities that live here.\n- **Lorebooks** — Scoped knowledge rules attached to this world.\n\n## Creating a World\n\n1. Navigate to **Worlds** from the main navigation.\n2. Click **Create New World**.\n3. Fill in the title and details.\n4. Add images using the multi-image picker.\n5. Click a world image to set it as the thumbnail.\n6. Attach scenarios, characters, and lorebooks from the dropdown selectors.\n7. Click **Save**.\n\n## Loading a World for Chat\n\n1. In the chat sidebar, click the **World** panel.\n2. Select a world from the list.\n3. The world's description and attached lorebooks are injected into the context window.\n\n<info>\nYou can only have **one** active world at a time. Loading a new world replaces the previous one.\n</info>\n\n## World Image Picker\n\n- Click the image area to open the file picker.\n- Select multiple images at once.\n- Click any image to set it as the **thumbnail** (highlighted with a border).\n- Click the trash icon to remove an image.\n\nThe thumbnail is what appears on the world grid card. All images are stored for your reference."
        },
        {
            id: 'scenarios-overview',
            title: 'Scenarios',
            content: "# Scenarios\n\nA **Scenario** is a specific situation, event, or story setup within a world. It provides the immediate context for a chat session.\n\n## What a Scenario Contains\n\n- **Title** — The scenario's name.\n- **Details** — The situation description, events, and current state.\n- **Images** — Visual references (multi-image picker with thumbnail).\n- **Characters** — Who is present in this scene.\n- **Lorebooks** — Knowledge specific to this scenario.\n\n## Scenario vs World Scope\n\n| Aspect | World | Scenario |\n|--------|-------|----------|\n| Lifetime | Permanent setting | Temporary situation |\n| Breadth | Entire universe | One scene or event |\n| Characters | Residents | Present participants |\n| Lore | Global rules | Scene-specific facts |\n\n## Creating a Scenario\n\n1. Navigate to **Scenarios** from the main navigation.\n2. Click **Create New Scenario**.\n3. Fill in the title and details.\n4. Add images using the multi-image picker.\n5. Attach characters and lorebooks.\n6. Optionally, attach the scenario to a world.\n7. Click **Save**.\n\n## Loading a Scenario\n\nScenarios can be loaded:\n- **Directly** from the Scenarios page for a standalone chat.\n- **Through a World** — load a world first, then attach its scenarios.\n- **Via the sidebar** in an active chat.\n\n<info>\nA scenario does not require a world. You can create and use standalone scenarios for one-off chats.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'characters-overview',
            title: 'Characters',
            content: "# Characters\n\nA **Character** is an AI persona with a name, description, personality, and visual identity.\n\n## What a Character Contains\n\n- **Name** — The character's display name.\n- **Description** — Personality, backstory, speaking style, and behavioral traits.\n- **Images** — Avatar images. Multi-image picker with thumbnail selection.\n- **Role** — System, user, or assistant designation.\n- **Metadata** — Tags, creator info, version.\n\n## Creating a Character\n\n1. Navigate to **Characters** from the main navigation.\n2. Click **Create New Character**.\n3. Enter the name.\n4. Write the description (this is the most important field — it shapes personality).\n5. Add avatar images using the multi-image picker.\n6. Click a thumbnail to set the primary avatar.\n7. Click **Save**.\n\n## Character Description Best Practices\n\n- Be specific: \"She speaks in short, clipped sentences\" is better than \"She is quiet.\"\n- Include motivation: What does the character want?\n- Define relationships: How do they feel about the user?\n- Add quirks: Habits, catchphrases, physical mannerisms.\n- Keep it under 2000 characters for optimal context usage.\n\n## Loading Characters Into Chat\n\n1. In the chat sidebar, open the **Character** panel.\n2. Click **Add Character**.\n3. Select from your character library.\n4. The character's description is injected into the system prompt.\n5. Multiple characters can be loaded simultaneously.\n\n## Replacing Character Images\n\nIn the sidebar character panel:\n1. Right-click a character's avatar.\n2. Select **Replace Image**.\n3. Choose a new image file.\n4. The avatar updates immediately.\n\n<info>\nCharacter images are for your immersion only. They are not sent to the AI model (unless the model supports vision and you attach the image to a message).\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'lorebooks-overview',
            title: 'Lorebooks',
            content: "# Lorebooks\n\nA **Lorebook** is a **scoped memory rule** that decides when specialized knowledge \"wakes up\" and enters the model's context.\n\nThink of it as a filing system: instead of dumping all world knowledge into every message, lorebooks activate only when relevant.\n\n## What a Lorebook Entry Contains\n\n- **Title** — The entry's name.\n- **Content** — The knowledge text (can be long or short).\n- **Scope** — World, Scenario, or Character.\n- **Triggers** — When and how the entry activates.\n- **Priority** — Importance relative to other entries.\n- **Injection** — How the content appears in context.\n\n## The Three Scopes\n\n| Scope | When It Activates | Example |\n|-------|-------------------|---------|\n| **World** | Any chat using this world | \"The capital city is Aethelgard.\" |\n| **Scenario** | Any chat using this scenario | \"The bridge is out due to the storm.\" |\n| **Character** | When this specific character is present | \"Elara is afraid of heights.\" |\n\n<info>\nScope controls **where** the entry lives. A world-scoped entry is part of the world's permanent knowledge. A character-scoped entry only matters when that character is in the chat.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'lorebooks-triggers',
            title: 'Lorebook Triggers',
            content: "# Lorebook Triggers\n\nTriggers determine **when** a lorebook entry activates. An entry only enters the context when its trigger conditions are met.\n\n## Trigger Phrases\n\nThe primary keywords that activate the entry. Comma-separated.\n\nExample: `Aethelgard, capital, king's city`\n\n## Secondary Triggers\n\nAdditional phrases that can activate the entry but with lower priority.\n\nExample: `throne, castle, royal`\n\n## Trigger Mode\n\n| Mode | Behavior |\n|------|----------|\n| **Exact Match** | The exact phrase must appear in the message. |\n| **Loose Match** | Any word in the phrase triggers the entry. |\n| **Semantic Match** | Conceptually related words trigger the entry (requires embedding model). |\n\n## Trigger Direction\n\n| Direction | Activates On |\n|-----------|--------------|\n| **User Only** | Your messages only. |\n| **Characters Only** | AI character messages only. |\n| **Both** | Any message. |\n\n## Trigger Frequency\n\n| Frequency | Behavior |\n|-----------|----------|\n| **Always** | Activates every time triggers match. |\n| **Once Per Chat** | Activates the first time triggers match, then never again. |\n| **Once Per Scene** | Activates once per scenario load. |\n| **Once Per Message** | Can activate multiple times, but only once per individual message. |\n\n## Example Setup\n\n**Title**: The Cursed Sword\n**Triggers**: `cursed sword, black blade, Vornath`\n**Direction**: Both\n**Frequency**: Always\n\nResult: Whenever anyone mentions the cursed sword, the entry's content is injected into the context."
        },
        {
            id: 'lorebooks-priority',
            title: 'Priority & Conflict Rules',
            content: "# Priority & Conflict Rules\n\nWhen multiple lorebook entries activate simultaneously, HOWL needs to decide which ones to include and which to skip.\n\n## Priority Level\n\n**Range**: 1 to 5\n**Default**: 3\n\n- **1** — Critical. Always included if triggered.\n- **3** — Standard. Default for most entries.\n- **5** — Minor. Included only if context budget allows.\n\n## Conflict Rules\n\nWhen two entries with the same trigger activate:\n\n| Rule | Behavior |\n|------|----------|\n| **Higher Priority** | The entry with the higher priority wins. |\n| **Newer Entry** | The most recently created entry wins. |\n| **Use Both** | Both entries are included (may exceed budget). |\n\n## Context Budget\n\nThe maximum number of tokens that all active lorebook entries can consume.\n\n- **160** → Conservative. Good default.\n- **320** → Generous. For lore-heavy worlds.\n- **640+** → Very lore-heavy. Reduces space for chat history.\n\n## Scan Window\n\nHow many recent messages are checked for triggers.\n\n- **4** → Checks last 4 messages. Default.\n- **8** → Checks last 8 messages. Better for slow-building triggers.\n- **16** → Checks last 16 messages. More responsive but slower.\n\n<warning>\nIf your context budget is too high, lorebooks will crowd out the chat history. If too low, important entries may be skipped. Start with 160 and adjust based on your world's complexity.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'lorebooks-injection',
            title: 'Injection Style & Position',
            content: "# Injection Style & Position\n\nControls **how** and **where** the lorebook content appears in the model's context.\n\n## Injection Style\n\n| Style | Behavior |\n|-------|----------|\n| **Inline** | Inserted directly into the message flow. |\n| **Summary** | Condensed into a brief summary. |\n| **Memory** | Formatted as a memory entry. |\n| **Background** | Added as part of the world description. |\n\n## Injection Position\n\n| Position | Behavior |\n|----------|----------|\n| **Before Message** | Prepended to the current message. |\n| **After Message** | Appended to the current message. |\n| **System Summary Only** | Added to the system prompt area. |\n| **Hidden** | Not shown to the model (for reference only). |\n\n## Visibility\n\n| Level | Behavior |\n|-------|----------|\n| **Public** | Visible to all characters and the user. |\n| **Private** | Only the user sees it (not sent to model). |\n| **Hidden** | Stored but never injected. |\n\n## Reveal Conditions\n\nControls when hidden or private entries become visible:\n\n| Condition | Behavior |\n|-----------|----------|\n| **Character Says It** | Revealed when an AI mentions it. |\n| **User Reveals It** | Revealed when you mention it. |\n| **Scenario Reveals It** | Revealed by scenario events. |\n| **Never Reveal** | Stays hidden permanently. |\n\n<info>\n**System Summary Only** is the most common position. It adds the entry to the system prompt area, making it available to all characters without cluttering the message flow.\n</info>"
        },
        {
            id: 'lorebooks-advanced',
            title: 'Advanced Lorebook Features',
            content: "# Advanced Lorebook Features\n\n## Max Length\n\nControls how much of the entry content is injected:\n\n| Length | Approximate Tokens |\n|--------|-------------------|\n| **Short** | ~50 tokens |\n| **Medium** | ~150 tokens |\n| **Full Entry** | Entire content |\n\nUse Short for quick references and Full Entry for detailed rules.\n\n## Linked Characters\n\nAttach the entry to specific characters. When those characters are present, the entry has higher activation priority.\n\n## Linked Lore Entries\n\nCreate chains of knowledge. Entry A can reference Entry B, and when A activates, B is more likely to activate too.\n\n## Inheritance\n\n| Mode | Behavior |\n|------|----------|\n| **None** | Standalone entry. |\n| **World** | Inherits from a world entry. |\n| **Scenario** | Inherits from a scenario entry. |\n| **Character** | Inherits from a character entry. |\n\nInherited entries share trigger phrases and priority with their parent.\n\n## Recursion\n\nControls whether entries can trigger other entries:\n\n- **None** → Entries only respond to chat messages.\n- **Limited** → Entries can trigger one level deep. Default.\n- **Full** → Entries can trigger chains. Risk of loops.\n\n<warning>\nFull recursion can cause infinite activation loops if entries reference each other. Use Limited unless you carefully design your trigger network.\n</warning>"
        },
        {
            id: 'lorebooks-best-practices',
            title: 'Lorebook Best Practices',
            content: "# Lorebook Best Practices\n\n## 1. Keep Triggers Specific\n\nBad: `magic` (triggers on every fantasy reference)\nGood: `blood magic ritual, Crimson Circle`\n\nSpecific triggers reduce false activations and keep context clean.\n\n## 2. Use Priority Wisely\n\n- **Priority 1**: Core world facts that must always be present.\n- **Priority 3**: Standard entries for characters, places, events.\n- **Priority 5**: Flavor text, minor details.\n\n## 3. Budget Your Context\n\nStart with **Context Budget: 160** and **Max Length: Medium**. Increase only if entries are being skipped too often.\n\n## 4. Organize by Scope\n\n- World scope: Geography, history, politics, magic systems.\n- Scenario scope: Current events, temporary conditions, scene NPCs.\n- Character scope: Personal secrets, backstory, relationships, phobias.\n\n## 5. Use System Summary Only\n\nFor most entries, **System Summary Only** is the best injection position. It keeps the message flow clean while making knowledge available.\n\n## 6. Test Triggers\n\nAfter creating entries, send test messages containing your trigger phrases. Verify they activate as expected.\n\n## 7. Avoid Redundancy\n\nIf two entries say similar things, merge them or use inheritance. Redundant entries waste context budget.\n\n## 8. Chain Linked Entries\n\nUse **Linked Lore Entries** to create knowledge webs:\n\n- Entry: \"The Black Tower\"\n  - Linked: \"Necromancer Zarthos\" (who lives there)\n  - Linked: \"Crimson Legion\" (who attacks it)\n\nWhen the tower is mentioned, all related knowledge becomes more likely to activate.\n\n<info>\nA well-designed lorebook feels invisible. The AI just \"knows\" things without you ever seeing the raw entries in the chat. That's the goal.\n</info>"
        }
    ]
};
